  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041314/assets/c21ae590fc35738a016fd9c6191bd15685d0d0a6fb043f32aafaf8d01845d149.png)\n\nThe graph above shows a portion of the curve $y=2\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x$ and the line $y=2\\sqrt{3}.$\nFind an expression of the form\n$x=\\theta +n\\cdot b,$\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ),$ $n$ is an integer and $b$ is the period in radians, that generates the $x$-coordinates of the points of intersection.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,n\\,\\cdot $\n{{field-2}}",
  "worked_solution": "At the intersection points, the $y$-values are the same for both graphs. This gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x=2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}\u2061x=\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent function has a period of $\\pi .$  Hence, the general solution for the given equation is \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\cdot \\pi .\n$$",
